  "answer_text": "Great question! That whitish film is most likely **limescale** (also called **mineral scale**), and it's very common. Here's a breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### What Is It?\n\nThe white residue you see on glassware is the result of minerals carried through your tap water, typically called **limescale**. The primary culprit is **calcium carbonate (CaCO\u2083)**, a naturally occurring mineral that is a key component of **hard water** \u2014 water that contains high concentrations of minerals like calcium and magnesium.\n\n---\n\n### Why Does It Happen Even With Filtered Water?\n\nMost municipal and well water contains dissolved calcium and magnesium ions. When water hardness reaches moderate to high levels, these minerals remain dissolved only as long as the water stays cool and stable. As water evaporates or sits over time, calcium carbonate and related mineral deposits form and adhere to surfaces like glass. Once these minerals have precipitated, they are no longer removable by simple rinsing because they are no longer dissolved in the water.\n\nStandard fridge filters are great at removing things like chlorine and some contaminants, but they don't typically remove dissolved minerals \u2014 so the limescale-forming minerals pass right through.\n\n---\n\n### Is It Dangerous?\n\nThe white film is a harmless mineral deposit, and while aesthetically unappealing, it poses **no health risks** and is a common occurrence.\n\n---\n\n### How Can You Test It?\n\nOne way to test whether the white particles are from hard water is to place a sample in a cup of vinegar. If the particles are from hard water, they will bubble up and dissolve into the vinegar. If they are plastic pieces, they will not dissolve at all.\n\n---\n\n### How Do You Clean It?\n\nYou can make your own cleaning solution by combining **equal parts vinegar and water**. Submerge the affected glassware in the solution and let it soak overnight for best results, then rinse with warm water until the limescale is gone.\n\nIn short, it's a completely natural and harmless buildup of minerals from your water \u2014 just one of the quirks of living in an area with hard water!",
